Signalbox Alerts: 
- New CLI command: `signalbox alerts` lists recent alerts triggered by scripts.
- Implementation steps:
  - Parse script logs/output for alert patterns defined in YAML config.
  - Support multiple alert patterns per script (alerts: list).
  - Store alerts with script name, message, timestamp, and status.
  - Display alert summary in CLI; allow filtering by time/severity.
- Consider storing alerts in a dedicated log or database for efficient querying.
- Design extensible pattern matching for future alert types.


Taskfile / justfile support (revisit later, discussed 2026-11-17; chose "option A")
- Goal: signalbox watches jobs whose logic lives in a Taskfile.yml or justfile (schedule, logs, alerts, tray), without becoming a task runner itself.
- Option A: a signalbox task points at a Task task instead of having a command:
      - name: infra-backup
        description: Nightly database backup
        taskfile: ~/infra/Taskfile.yml
        task: backup
        alerts: [...]          # signalbox settings stay next to the reference
    - signalbox builds the command at run time: task --taskfile <abs path> -x --silent <task>, cwd = the Taskfile's folder, shlex-quoted
        - -x (--exit-code): pass on the real exit code (without it a failure is always Task's 201)
        - --silent: Task does not print each command, so unmarked secrets do not end up in signalbox logs
    - a task has either command or taskfile+task, never both (validator)
    - validate: run task --taskfile F --list-all --json once per Taskfile; warn if the file, the task or the task binary is missing
      (only in validate; at run time a missing task exits 200 and shows as failed/red anyway)
    - check-permissions: also check each referenced Taskfile (whoever writes it decides what runs)
    - justfile: same with justfile: + recipe:, command just --justfile F --working-directory D RECIPE (just already passes exit codes)
        - recipes with parameters that have no default cannot run unattended: validate warns
    - tests: fake task/just scripts on PATH that print fixed JSON and record their arguments (like the crontab --install test)
    - about 20-30 lines plus tests
- Docs to write with it:
    - Task's own per-command timeout: should be shorter than signalbox's, so Task can stop the command and run its defer cleanup (signalbox's SIGKILL skips defer)
    - tasks with prompt: fail at once (stdin is /dev/null); never add --yes, those prompts protect something
    - parallel deps: suggest --output prefixed in a hand-written command if logs get mixed
    - remote includes change what runs without a signalbox config change: recommend checksum: pinning; SECURITY.md paragraph
    - one Task task = one signalbox job: a Task deps chain runs inside one signalbox run, with one log and one status
    - only runs that go through signalbox are recorded, not a manual "task backup" in a terminal
- Not doing:
    - signalbox import taskfile (copying entries): option A makes it unnecessary
    - live loading of whole Taskfiles (taskfiles: in signalbox.yaml): noise from dev tasks, no place for alerts/timeouts,
      renamed tasks vanish quietly, every config load depends on task, weaker review model
- Legal: go-task is MIT, just is CC0. Calling their CLIs and borrowing ideas is fine; copied code keeps the MIT notice. Say "works with Task", no logos or implied endorsement.


Features to borrow from Task (discussed 2026-11-17, ranked by fit; our own implementation, so no legal issue)
- DONE 2026-11-17: env: and env_file: per task (prepare_run in executor.py builds command, cwd, env, secrets)
- DONE 2026-11-17: stop tasks gracefully: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after execution.stop_grace_seconds (default 5)
- DONE 2026-11-17: secret masking in logs: variables listed in mask:, plus the task's own variables whose
  names have TOKEN/SECRET/PASS/KEY/AUTH/CREDENTIAL/SIGNATURE (masking every env_file value hid ordinary settings)
- Preconditions that skip instead of fail (drive mounted, online, VPN up): field run_if: (a command or a list)
    - new status "skipped: condition not met", grey/neutral in the tray instead of red
- Overlap protection: field overlap: skip | wait | allow, DEFAULT skip (decided 2026-11-17; changes current behaviour, note it in the changelog)
    - lock: fcntl.flock on logs/<task>/.run.lock for the whole run; skip = non-blocking, wait = up to the task's time limit
- Warning on repeated skips (decided 2026-11-17): count-based, one global setting
    - e.g. execution.skip_warn_after: 3 (0 = never warn); the tray turns yellow and status exits 1 after that many skips in a row
    - runtime keeps a consecutive skip count per task; any real run (success or failure) resets it
    - run_if and overlap skips count; platform skips do not (the task is hidden on that machine)
    - reasoning: well-planned tasks rarely skip, so repeated skips mean something is wrong
- Dry run: signalbox task run / group run --dry-run prints commands, cwd, time limits, runs nothing (also listed in SECURITY.md future)
- Machine-readable output: signalbox status --json, task list --json, group list --json (Task's issue #764 shows the demand)
- platforms: [darwin, linux] on a task, for one config shared across machines
- Not borrowing: templating/loops, up-to-date checks (sources/generates), watch mode, includes, remote config
